WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:02.980
SARA ROBINSON: Welcome back to
the last video in our What-if

00:00:02.980 --> 00:00:03.700
Tool miniseries.

00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:05.867
In the first video, James
and I gave you an overview

00:00:05.867 --> 00:00:07.280
of the What-if Tool.

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:09.330
And in the second video,
we did a deep dive

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:10.800
of the Datapoint Editor.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:14.220
Here, we'll be focusing on
the Performance &amp; Fairness tab

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:17.460
using the same housing data
set from the last two videos.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:19.920
This model predicts whether
a house is worth more or less

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:21.900
than $160,000.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:22.680
Let's get started.

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.159
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:28.598 --> 00:00:30.890
JAMES WEXLER: The first thing
we see on the Performance

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.840
&amp; Fairness tab are various
performance metrics

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:33.665
for our model.

00:00:33.665 --> 00:00:35.720
The confusion matrix
tells us the percentage

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:37.190
of each class our
model classified

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:39.192
correctly and incorrectly.

00:00:39.192 --> 00:00:41.150
If we add the numbers on
the top left diagonal,

00:00:41.150 --> 00:00:46.040
it will equal our overall model
accuracy-- in this case, 97%.

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:48.710
This shows us that 50 out of 51
data points from our test set

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:51.760
were correctly classified
as over $160,000,

00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:54.410
and 47 out of 49 data
points from our test set

00:00:54.410 --> 00:00:57.590
were correctly classified
as less than $160,000.

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:00.720
The model had very few false
negatives and false positives.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.080
So far, we haven't changed
the threshold slider.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.879
Sara, do you want to explain
what we can do with it?

00:01:04.879 --> 00:01:05.570
SARA ROBINSON: Sure.

00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.730
The threshold indicates
your model's decision point

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:09.020
between classes.

00:01:09.020 --> 00:01:11.570
In this case, any
prediction above 0.5

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:15.230
will be classified as
more than $160,000.

00:01:15.230 --> 00:01:17.120
Changing the threshold
is one technique

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:19.340
for optimizing your
model's performance.

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.130
To change it in the What-if
Tool, we can adjust the slider.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.100
When we do this, you'll notice
that our metrics change.

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:26.960
In this case,
increasing the threshold

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:30.020
decreases overall accuracy
but also decreases

00:01:30.020 --> 00:01:31.297
our false positives.

00:01:31.297 --> 00:01:33.380
JAMES WEXLER: If we select
a single threshold from

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:35.900
the optimization strategies,
the What-if Tool will choose

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.270
the threshold that yields
the highest overall accuracy

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.810
on the loaded data points--

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.980
so the lowest total of false
negatives and false positives.

00:01:42.980 --> 00:01:45.220
For this model,
it's still a 0.5.

00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:47.720
This view shows us how our model
is behaving across all data

00:01:47.720 --> 00:01:48.220
points.

00:01:48.220 --> 00:01:49.678
But what if we want
to see how it's

00:01:49.678 --> 00:01:51.553
behaving across different
slices of our data?

00:01:51.553 --> 00:01:53.178
That will help us
understand if there's

00:01:53.178 --> 00:01:55.190
areas where it's treating
subsets of data points

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:56.210
differently.

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:58.910
Sara, do you want to show us how
to slice by different features?

00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.110
SARA ROBINSON: Sure.

00:02:00.110 --> 00:02:02.540
Let's try slicing by
garage type attached,

00:02:02.540 --> 00:02:04.100
which indicates
whether the garage is

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:06.260
attached to the house.

00:02:06.260 --> 00:02:08.120
Notice that houses
with an attached garage

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:10.789
have a higher likelihood that
our model will value them

00:02:10.789 --> 00:02:14.480
at more than $160,000.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:17.060
Now let's imagine that we
wanted our model to price houses

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.650
with attached and unattached
garages in the same way.

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:22.400
In this example, we
care most about having

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:26.510
the same percentage of positive
classifications across slices

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.000
while still achieving the
highest possible accuracy

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:31.730
within that constraint.

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:33.890
For this, we should
select demographic parity

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:36.440
as our optimization strategy
from the fairness section

00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:39.383
on the bottom left.

00:02:39.383 --> 00:02:41.300
Notice that our threshold
sliders and accuracy

00:02:41.300 --> 00:02:43.785
metrics change when
we set this strategy.

00:02:43.785 --> 00:02:45.660
JAMES WEXLER: What do
all these changes mean?

00:02:45.660 --> 00:02:46.880
If we don't want
the garage placement

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.328
to influence our
model's price, we

00:02:48.328 --> 00:02:50.120
need to use different
thresholds for houses

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.730
depending on whether
the garage is attached.

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.690
With these updated
thresholds, the model

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:57.800
will predict the house to
be worth more than $160,000

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:00.620
when the prediction
score is 0.99 or higher.

00:03:00.620 --> 00:03:02.950
Alternatively, a house
without an attached garage

00:03:02.950 --> 00:03:05.060
should be classified
as over $160,000

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.120
if the model predicts
0.52 or higher.

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:10.587
What about other
optimization strategies?

00:03:10.587 --> 00:03:12.920
SARA ROBINSON: We can use the
equal opportunity strategy

00:03:12.920 --> 00:03:15.410
to achieve a similar percentage
of correct predictions

00:03:15.410 --> 00:03:18.030
across each slice for the
houses in our test data

00:03:18.030 --> 00:03:21.530
set that are worth
more than $160,000.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:24.620
And equal accuracy will
strive for similar accuracy

00:03:24.620 --> 00:03:26.810
across both slices.

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.120
Finally, one more performance
and fairness feature

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.490
we should look at is
changing the cost ratio.

00:03:31.490 --> 00:03:33.320
JAMES WEXLER: So far, all of
our optimization strategies

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:34.670
have tried to pick
the best thresholds,

00:03:34.670 --> 00:03:36.770
given that we care equally
about false positives

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.300
and false negatives.

00:03:38.300 --> 00:03:40.700
But this may not be the
case for many models.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:43.233
For a model used in cancer
screening, for example,

00:03:43.233 --> 00:03:45.650
you might want the model to
avoid false negatives, meaning

00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:47.660
misdiagnoses, at
the cost of having

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:50.120
more false positives, which
could then be screened out

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.362
at the next level of testing.

00:03:51.362 --> 00:03:52.820
SARA ROBINSON: To
update thresholds

00:03:52.820 --> 00:03:55.237
based on how you want your
model to handle false positives

00:03:55.237 --> 00:03:57.980
and negatives, you can
change the cost ratio.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:04:00.670
For example, setting
the cost ratio to 4

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:03.380
means that we want to consider
false negatives four times

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:05.540
as costly as false positives.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:08.180
Then we can find the optimal
threshold, given this new cost

00:04:08.180 --> 00:04:08.880
ratio.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:10.880
JAMES WEXLER: We've covered
a lot in this video.

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.470
To recap, we looked
at a few metrics

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:14.220
for evaluating the
performance of a model,

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:16.519
including the confusion
matrix, and true and false

00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:17.293
positive rates.

00:04:17.293 --> 00:04:19.460
Then we looked at modifying
the prediction threshold

00:04:19.460 --> 00:04:22.220
and analyzed how our model
behaved across different slices

00:04:22.220 --> 00:04:22.720
of our data.

00:04:22.720 --> 00:04:24.512
SARA ROBINSON: Keep in
mind that while this

00:04:24.512 --> 00:04:25.940
is a data set of
houses, we'd want

00:04:25.940 --> 00:04:27.770
to do a similar
analysis for other types

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:30.680
of data, especially
human-centered data.

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.450
It's also important
to note that we've

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:35.360
shown just one strategy for
improving the fairness of an ML

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:36.300
model.

00:04:36.300 --> 00:04:38.300
There are many other
methods available for this,

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:40.730
such as augmenting
training data and designing

00:04:40.730 --> 00:04:43.103
fairer cost functions
for use at training time.

00:04:43.103 --> 00:04:45.020
JAMES WEXLER: For more
resources on everything

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:47.400
we showed, check out the links
in the video description.

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:48.233
Thanks for watching.

00:04:48.233 --> 00:04:51.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

